course: dictionary
lesson: 9060
title: Slice: one word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A slice is a thin flat piece cut from something, like bread or cake. «A loaf has a set number of slices, and a shop sells only whole loaves.» Here a slice is one piece of a whole loaf, so the loaf is the whole and each slice is one part of it.
explain: Slice is also a verb: to slice means to cut something into pieces like that. When you read «slice up», ask yourself what the word does. Put your meaning into the sentence, "cut into pieces", and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Slice can also mean a part or share of something, as in «a slice of the money». Nothing is being cut into food here. Read the words around it: is an actual object being cut, or is something being shared out? The context tells you which meaning you need.
explain: Sport uses slice too. To slice a ball means to hit it so it spins and curves. In the kitchen, always slice with care: use a board, keep your fingers away from the blade, and ask an adult for sharp knives.
fact: A slice is a thin, flat piece cut from something, such as bread, cake, meat or pizza.
fact: To slice means to cut something into pieces like that.
fact: A slice of something is a part or share of it, as in «a slice of the money».
fact: In sport, to slice a ball means to hit it so it spins and curves.
fact: Holes in bread are where gas bubbles were trapped while the dough rose.
exercise: «A loaf has a set number of slices, and a shop sells only whole loaves.» What is a slice? | A kind of knife | *A thin flat piece cut from something like bread | A whole loaf sold in a shop | A bubble in the dough
exercise: Ben says, «He wants a slice of the money.» What does slice mean here? | To cut into pieces | A thin piece of bread | To hit a ball so it spins | *A part or share of it
exercise: «Mum will slice up the cake for the party.» Find and copy the word that means cut into pieces. | Mum | *slice | party | cake
test: In sport, what does it mean to slice a ball? | To kick it into the net | To catch it with both hands | To throw it very high | *To hit it so it spins and curves
test: «Every hole in a slice of bread is where a bubble once was.» What do the holes show? | Where someone pushed a finger in | Where the bread was burnt black | Where mould grew on the bread | *Where gas was trapped while the dough rose
exam: Lily wants to slice bread at home. What is the safest way? | Hold the loaf in one hand and cut towards your fingers | *Use a board, cut with care, and keep your fingers away from the blade | Cut while you run around the kitchen | Use any knife you find, without asking an adult
